---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import MapLinks from '../components/MapLinks.astro';
import ArticleCard from '../components/ArticleCard.astro';
import PhotoGrid from '../components/PhotoGrid.astro';
import ChannelCards from '../components/ChannelCards.astro';
import StartHere from '../components/StartHere.astro';
import DiscordCTA from '../components/DiscordCTA.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { publishedArticles } from '../lib/help';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
// The SDR waterfall lives outside src/assets/photos/ so the grid does not pick it up.
import sdrWaterfall from '../assets/sdr-waterfall.jpg';

const latest = (await publishedArticles()).slice(0, 3);

---

<BaseLayout title={site.name} description={site.tagline}>
  <Hero />

  <Section
    id="about"
    eyebrow="About"
    title="About SoCal Mesh"
    lede="No cell towers. No internet. Just radios, and the people who run them."
  >
    <div class="blocks">
      <article>
        <h3>What is SoCal Mesh?</h3>
        <p>
          SoCal Mesh is a community of individuals working to build a network of Meshtastic
          radios in Southern California. This network acts like an area-wide text messaging
          system, allowing people to communicate publicly or privately with anyone on the
          network. The mesh network can also be used to transmit GPS locations or read weather
          and other telemetry. All of this happens without any external infrastructure. No
          cell phone towers, no internet. We have hundreds of nodes and are growing every day.
          Please join us and feel free to ask questions on our{' '}
          <a href={url(site.discord.path)}>Discord</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>What is Meshtastic?</h3>
        <p>
          Meshtastic is an open source, decentralized, off-grid mesh network. It is made up of
          small communicators that can form a mesh network. These communicators can be linked
          to a phone via Bluetooth or Wi-Fi. Meshtastic uses inexpensive LoRa radios as a
          long-range communication platform. Learn more at the{' '}
          <a href={site.meshtastic.site} rel="noopener">official Meshtastic website</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          People across Southern California run nodes. Some are at home, some sit on hilltops, some
          live in a backpack. The radios talk to each other over LoRa in the 915 MHz band used in
          the US. The exact frequency comes from the preset and frequency slot you set, which is why
          it matters that we all use the same ones. They mesh using the open source Meshtastic
          firmware. You pair your own node to your phone over Bluetooth or Wi-Fi, and the node
          handles the radio side. Messages hop from node to node, so the network reaches further
          than any single radio can, and it keeps working when one node drops off.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="channels"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Channels"
    title="The channels we run"
    lede="Start on LongFast, frequency slot 20. It is the factory default and it is where many of us are. MediumFast slot 45 is the alternate."
  >
    <ChannelCards />

    <div class="why">
      <div>
        <h3>Start on LongFast, slot 20</h3>
        <p>
          This is the factory default and it is where many of us are. Set your region to US and a
          new radio is already on it, because LongFast with the slot left alone resolves to 20. For
          most people that means there is nothing to change here at all.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>MediumFast slot 45 is the alternate</h3>
        <p>
          Plenty of us in SoCal use it, and it is faster on the air. The same message takes less
          airtime, so a busy channel fills up more slowly. It is not always strong though, and
          whether it works for you depends on your area. Try it if you can connect on it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>LongTurbo slot 14 is an experiment</h3>
        <p>
          A wider 500 kHz channel. It carries a little more than LongFast for roughly 3 dB less
          reach, and slot 14 is the slot it settles on by itself, the same way LongFast settles on
          20. We are only starting to test it, and we think it ends up as our default here in time.
          You need firmware 2.7.17 or newer, and older phone apps may not list the preset yet. Worth
          adding if you want to help test. Do not make it the only channel on your only radio.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>How to tell what is near you</h3>
        <p>
          Check <a href={site.maps[0].url} rel="noopener">our live map</a> for MediumFast slot 45
          nodes around you. Remember a node only reaches a map if its owner switched on OK to
          MQTT, so there are always more nodes on the air than any map displays. An empty patch is
          not proof nobody is there. Some of why MF45 can be weaker is physics: it has about 5 dB less link budget than
          LongFast, so each hop reaches less far and the channel leans on having enough nodes nearby.
          Asking on Discord is the surer answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="sources">
      Where these numbers come from: Meshtastic's own{' '}
      <a href="https://meshtastic.org/docs/overview/radio-settings/" rel="noopener">radio settings
      reference</a>{' '}(the presets, their speeds and their link budgets). The case for MediumFast
      is argued by Meshtastic itself in{' '}
      <a href="https://meshtastic.org/blog/why-your-mesh-should-switch-from-longfast/" rel="noopener"
        >Why your mesh should switch from LongFast</a>, and by the communities that have moved.{' '}
      <a href="https://bayme.sh/docs/getting-started/recommended-settings/" rel="noopener">Bay Area
      Mesh</a> and <a href="https://mtnme.sh/mediumfast/" rel="noopener">MtnMe.sh</a> are both on
      MediumFast slot 45. It is a genuine argument. It just leans on having enough nodes near you,
      and that is the part that varies across Southern California.
    </p>

    <p class="warn">
      Set <strong>both</strong> the preset and the frequency slot. Type the slot number in rather
      than leaving it at 0. On 0 the radio works its frequency out from the primary channel's name,
      which is fine on a factory-fresh radio and stops being fine the moment anything has renamed
      that channel. A LongFast radio also cannot hear a MediumFast one at all, whatever the slot
      says, because the preset has to match too.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="start"
    eyebrow="New here?"
    title="Start here"
    lede="From an unopened radio to your first message on the mesh."
  >
    <StartHere />
    <p class="more">
      <Button href={url(site.discord.path)} variant="accent">Ask us on Discord</Button>
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="maps"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Maps"
    title="See the network"
    lede="Four views of the mesh. None of them shows all of it."
  >
    <MapLinks />

    <p class="warn">
      A node only reaches these maps if its owner switched on <strong>OK to MQTT</strong>, which is
      permission rather than an upload: it lets any gateway that hears you pass your traffic on.
      Plenty of people never turn it on, so there are always more nodes on the air than the maps
      show. Empty ground means nobody there is uplinking, not that nobody is there.{' '}
      <a href={url('/help/how-do-i-uplink-to-mqtt/')}>How to turn it on</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="help"
    eyebrow="Help"
    title="Getting started"
    lede="Short guides written by people in the mesh."
  >
    {
      latest.length > 0 ? (
        <>
          <div class="cards">
            {latest.map((article) => (
              <ArticleCard
                slug={article.id}
                title={article.data.title}
                description={article.data.description}
                author={article.data.author}
                date={article.data.date}
                updated={article.data.updated}
              />
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="more">
            <a href={url('/help/')}>All help articles →</a>
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p class="lede">
          No articles yet. Ask on <a href={url(site.discord.path)}>Discord</a> and we
          will write one.
        </p>
      )
    }
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="photos"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Photos"
    title="Out in the field"
    lede="Nodes, antennas, meetups, and a waterfall full of LoRa."
  >
    <PhotoGrid />
    <div class="sdr">
      <Image
        src={sdrWaterfall}
        alt="An SDR waterfall display showing bursts of LoRa traffic near 906.9 MHz"
        widths={[480, 800, 1200, 1600]}
        width={1600}
        height={900}
        sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 60rem, 92vw"
        format="webp"
        quality={72}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <p class="caption">LoRa traffic on the 33 cm band, seen from an SDR.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="discord">
    <DiscordCTA />
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="community"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Community"
    title="Projects and links"
    lede="Everything we run is open source."
  >
    <ul class="links">
      {
        site.communityLinks.map((link) => (
          <li>
            <a href={link.url} rel="noopener">
              {link.name}
            </a>
            <p>{link.blurb}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="sources">
      We also lean on a handful of open source projects that are not ours, including the software
      behind our map and the parrot in our Discord. They are listed and thanked on the{' '}
      <a href={url('/links/')}>links page</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="more">
      <Button href={url(site.discord.path)} variant="accent">Join us on Discord</Button>
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .blocks {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-l);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 20rem), 1fr));
  }

  .blocks h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .blocks p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
  }

  .sdr {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
  }

  .sdr img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .caption {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }

  .why {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 17rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-m);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
  }

  .why h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
  }

  .why p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .sources {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-m);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .warn {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin: var(--space-m) 0 0;
    padding: var(--space-s) var(--space-m);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .warn strong {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .links {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .links li {
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-m);
  }

  .links a {
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .links p {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
</style>
